//
//! The build half of libjpeg-turbo's runtime-loading route: three modules and
//! one generated file.
//!
//! An SDK that ships the runtime route calls `createModule` in place of
//! `translate.createModule()` and imports what comes back as its `c` module.
//! Nothing here links the native library. That is the point: in this
//! configuration the symbols arrive through the platform loader at run time,
//! and linking the archive as well would make the linker demand them before
//! `load` ever runs.
//!
//!   rt        runtime/loader.zig, the reusable core. Pure Zig; imported by the
//!             facade in both configurations, because `LoadSource` and
//!             `LoadError` are this SDK's public types and must be the same
//!             types whichever configuration is built.
//!   library   runtime/library.zig, this SDK's descriptor: the namespaced
//!             synchronization binding and the one load-time check.
//!   dispatch  generated by runtime/generate.zig from the real translation
//!             output: the table, the wrappers, `load`, and the re-exported
//!             translated namespace. It imports `rt`, `library` and the
//!             generator's patched translation as `c_native`, which imports the
//!             dispatch back as `c_routed`.
//!   sync.c    compiled into the dispatch with `-DRT_SYNC_PREFIX=jpeg_turbo_rt`,
//!             so its symbols cannot collide with another SDK's.
//!
//! ## The one thing a caller must not do
//!
//! Do not link the compiled C archive into a module that imports this dispatch.
//! The facade's `c` import is the dispatch, and nothing else in the graph should
//! reference the library, or the linker resolves the symbols statically and the
//! runtime route silently becomes a linked one.
//!
//! ## What is routed, and what is not
//!
//! The translated header for this SDK is `zig/translate.h`, which names exactly
//! the four installed headers - `jconfig.h`, `jmorecfg.h`, `jpeglib.h` and
//! `jerror.h` (`src/CMakeLists.txt:2065-2069`) - and defines no macro of its
//! own. Those headers declare 66 functions: every `EXTERN(...)` line in
//! `src/src/jpeglib.h`, all of them named `jpeg_` or `jpeg12_`/`jpeg16_`. There
//! is no exported data object among them, and there is no config-gated entry
//! point: `MEM_SRCDST_SUPPORTED` is an unconditional `#define`
//! (`src/src/jconfig.h.in:18-19`) and the `#cmakedefine` capability lines
//! (`:13`, `:16`, `:22`) gate internal behaviour rather than declarations -
//! `jpeg12_*` and `jpeg16_*` are declared unconditionally
//! (`src/src/jpeglib.h:1025-1029`, `:1079-1084`) and refuse at run time on an
//! 8-bit build. Every one of those 66 names is therefore routed, and a provider
//! that does not export the whole header-declared API is refused rather than
//! half used.
//!
//! ## The C library's `stdio` surface, and why it is the only noise here
//!
//! `src/src/jpeglib.h:21-29` says in as many words that it does *not* include
//! `<stdio.h>`, "despite the fact that it uses `FILE` and `size_t`", and that
//! calling programs are required to include system headers that define at
//! least those two typedefs before including it. So `zig/translate.h` opens
//! `<stddef.h>` and `<stdio.h>` itself, and *that* is the only route by which
//! any C library header enters this translation:
//!
//!   * `src/src/jconfig.h` is generated from `src/src/jconfig.h.in` and opens
//!     no header.
//!   * `src/src/jmorecfg.h` opens no header at all; it is typedefs and macros
//!     from `:49` to `:389`.
//!   * `src/src/jerror.h` opens no header either.
//!   * `src/src/jpeglib.h:41-44` includes `jconfig.h` and `jmorecfg.h`, and
//!     its `jpegint.h`/`jerror.h` tail at `:1212-1215` is behind
//!     `JPEG_INTERNALS`, which this wrapper leaves undefined.
//!
//! `zig translate-c` emits a declaration for every function it parses in that
//! include closure, whether or not this build calls it, so the translation
//! carries the target libc's whole `stdio` surface alongside libjpeg-turbo's
//! own. Those are the C library's symbols, not the provider's: a `libjpeg.so`
//! does not export `printf`, and asking a provider for one would either fail
//! the load outright or, worse, resolve it out of the C library the provider
//! itself depends on. They are therefore classified as noise, which keeps the
//! declarations direct.
//!
//! ## The list below, and what it is a union of
//!
//! The list is the union, over the libc families this package can be configured
//! for, of every function and object name that reaches the translation through
//! the include closure of `<stdio.h>` (and `<stddef.h>`, which adds no
//! declaration of its own). It is deliberately *broader* than this package
//! needs: it also carries names that arrive through `<stdarg.h>`,
//! `<limits.h>` and `<stdint.h>`, which the Lua package's equivalent closure
//! does reach and this one does not.
//!
//! Being broader is the safe direction and the reasoning is the same on both
//! sides of it: a name a given target's libc does not declare simply never
//! appears in that target's translation, so the entry costs nothing, while a
//! name that is missing from this list would be routed and would turn every
//! load into a `MissingSymbol` on a symbol that plainly belongs to the C
//! library. The list is therefore a superset rather than a re-derivation.
//!
//! **PENDING-VERIFICATION.** No `zig translate-c` has been run for this
//! package: no gate has been run for it at all. The list is carried from the
//! Lua package's `<stdio.h>` closure classification, which was read out of that
//! package's real translations for its six `check_targets` and for every target
//! of the published 53-platform matrix. The first observed `zig build -Druntime`
//! on a given target settles whether any name is missing here, and a missing
//! name shows up as a `MissingSymbol` naming it, not as a silent pass.
//!
//! The families are the ones `lib/libc/include` holds in the pinned Zig
//! distribution for a target this package can be built for:
//! `generic-glibc`, `generic-musl`, `wasm-wasi-musl`, `any-windows-any`,
//! `any-darwin-any`, `generic-freebsd`, `generic-netbsd` and `generic-openbsd`.
//! The names below were read out of real translations rather than out of the
//! headers by hand; a name that is a preprocessor macro rather than a
//! declaration cannot appear in a translation at all, because a macro cannot
//! survive as a declaration.
//!
//! What the generated headers do not change: `WITH_ARITH_ENC`,
//! `WITH_ARITH_DEC`, `WITH_PROFILE` and `WITH_SIMD` are `#cmakedefine` lines in
//! `src/src/jconfig.h.in` (`:13`, `:16`, `:22`, `:58`) that this build resolves
//! to absent, and re-running the translation with or without each of them
//! answers the same set of names, because what they select are branches in
//! upstream's `.c` units - `src/src/jccolor.c`, `src/src/jchuff.c`,
//! `src/src/jcsample.c`, `src/src/jdcolor.c`, `src/src/jdsample.c` - and the
//! translation reads headers only.

const std = @import("std");

/// The prefix `runtime/sync.c` is compiled with.
///
/// `runtime/library.zig` declares the same prefix in the symbol names it binds.
/// The two must agree, and a build where they do not fails to link, which is
/// the whole error report: there is no runtime path that could hide it.
///
/// A build that needs a second, independent instance of this SDK in one binary
/// should change this value *and* the prefix in `runtime/library.zig`, and then
/// give the second instance its own copy of both files. Nothing else in the
/// package assumes one instance.
const sync_prefix = "jpeg_turbo_rt";

/// `extern` declarations that are the C library's, not libjpeg-turbo's.
///
/// See "What is routed, and what is not" above for where this list comes from,
/// why it is a union, and why it is currently broader than this package's own
/// closure needs.
///
/// ### The list is comma-separated, and every line ends with one
///
/// `runtime/generate.zig` reads these three lists through
/// `std.mem.splitScalar(u8, list, ',')` followed by a whitespace trim, and a
/// Zig multiline string literal concatenates its lines with nothing between
/// them. A line that does not end in a comma therefore glues its last name to
/// the first name of the next line into one token, and that token matches
/// neither declaration: both real names are lost and both get routed. The Lua
/// package's copy of this file had exactly that fault - all ninety-one content
/// lines ended without a delimiter - and it is why a load there failed with
/// `MissingSymbol` on names such as `dprintf`, `ferror` and `fgetpos` that are
/// plainly the C library's. The commas are load-bearing, not decoration.
///
/// ### The fortified and alias declarations an optimized translation adds
///
/// `translate-c` reads the target's headers under the *build's*
/// configuration, and an optimized build turns `_FORTIFY_SOURCE` on. glibc's
/// `_FORTIFY_SOURCE` block declares a second family of externs an unfortified
/// pass never sees: a `__*_chk` implementation, a `__*_alias` redirect back onto
/// the plain function, and a `__*_chk_warn` sibling. They are declared by the C
/// library, the C library exports them, and `libjpeg` exports none of them.
